Assuming my default playstyle (wishful, no artifact wishes, modest
polypiling):

AC -22 or so (32 points) is plenty good enough to start Gehennom,
and achievable with only modest levels of enchantment and the "easy"
points of donation protection.  32 points of AC can be had from 13
points of base AC (assuming 9-point DSM, 1-point cloak, 1-point helm,
1-point boots, 1-point gloves, 0-point shirt), 9 points of donation,
and 10 points of enchantment.

[If you use a shield for reflection (+2 pts) and GDSM for magic
resistance in order to use a cloak of protection (+2 pts), forgo the
use of your helmet slot for telepathy or brilliance in favour of a
dwarvish iron helm (+1 pt), and forgo continuous Very Fast-ness in
favour of iron shoes (+1 pt), that goes down to 4 points.]

The main dungeon should have yielded several scrolls of enchant armor,
and unless you were very unlucky up to the castle, you should be
able to spare at least one wish for a magic marker, which will get
you a few more.  Polypiling unicorn horns may get you another marker.
Getting 10 points of enchantment by the time you start in on Gehennom
is therefore trivial.

Clearing Gehennom is a bit dull, but if you do so, you will end up
with a few more scrolls of enchant armour and conceivably another
magic marker or two - maybe even another wand of wishing.  This gives
you a good chance of being able to raise all your armour pieces to +4.
Some of your armour may end up +5; anyone can get elven armour to +6
(maybe +7); and a wizard can get a cornuthaum to +6 (maybe +7).

All this will get you well past -30; if you're using a shield,
it'll get you past -40.  -40 is more than Good Enough for the late
game, given that experienced players do it with -20.  Prayers for
protection-by-pat-on-the-head are the cherry on top.
